review:
I can say this game is awesome if you you are a real fan of the franchise. If you you don't consider yourself one, you might find this game boring, obsolete, etc. It's a really good entry to the series and sticks to its traditions, has some interesting lore, but no more. It has not much to bring to the table in comparison with videogames overall. So just buy it if you really like Resident Evil.

Pros:
+ this game has its own weaponry upgrade system that's simpler than the one in RE 4 because you can just put all the bonuses from one weapon to another and change your build anytime you see a workbench, but it's still pretty deep and encourages you to build your own gunplay;
+ easy to use inventory and healing. Much better than in RE 6;
+ a new scanning mechanic allows you to scan enemies multiple times to get healing herbs. Some enemies are easy to scan, some are not because their corpses disappear quickly after death and you have to be fast or sometimes risk your life and scan during combat;
+ interesting enemy design. The action takes places on a ship and the monsters have kind of fish-themed appearances which makes this game stand out from other franchise entries. But some classic monsters are in place as well (for example, hunters and vicious dogs/wolfs);
+ [spoiler] the only RE game I know where you can swim under water [/spoiler];
+ classic RE gameplay with exploring locations, finding keys to rooms, solving puzzles;
+ a decent plot;
+ short side chapters where you have to play as other characters in different locations rather than the ship. Pretty good to add variety to the gameplay so you don't get bored;
+ the game gives you more Magnum ammo than some other series entries and encourages you to use it more;
+ interesting and unique enemies, especially closer to the end of the game;
+ a final boss with unique mechanics that are not typical for the Resident Evil series. I didn't even get how to beat and dodge him the first time I challenged him. But the fight turned out to be quite exclusive for the series. I know many people disliked it but hey, do you really like it when every boss is just a bullet sponge with glowing eyes all over his body? Come on, it's fine to get something different sometimes.

Cons:
- inconsistent graphics quality. Characters look OK, but the environments aren't. It's a common issue for all the games of the franchise, but here I suspected that Jill's ass contains as many polygons as the whole ship;
- aiming is a bit weird. A little stiff. I turned off the aim assist in the settings and it got better;
- clunky animations and movements of the most of the caharacters and enemies. Painful to see them nowadays. I guess it's because the original game was on 3DS;
- puzzles aren't that inventive as in RE 1-2. Here you will mostly see only searching for keys, power restoring, lever pulling, gear insertion, you know, the most basic and generic stuff;
- it was hard for me to get used to the dodging controls. But game requires you to adapt to them early because [spoiler]there's a segment in the beginning when you are unarmed[/spoiler].
- some bosses are re-used;
- you have to press a button to come through each door, even if your companion has already opened it right in front of your face;
- sometimes you have to wait for your companion to enter the elevator so you don't leave him at the previous floor;
- loading between some areas takes too long even with an SSD;
- the game is too short. I wish it had more big levels to explore than just one ship.
- you are unlikely to experience this problem, but I keep my Steam in English and it downloads all the games in English. I live in a region where Russian is a primary language and, unlike others, this game decided to be in Russian for some reason. The localization is bad, it has typos and mistranslations. Sometimes there is mixed text in both English and Russian.
